.\" Automatically generated by Pandoc 3.2
.\"
.TH "rofi" "1" "" "rofi" "General Commands Manual"
.SH "نام (NAME)"
\f[B]rofi\f[R] \- A window switcher, application launcher, ssh dialog,
dmenu replacement and more
.SH "خلاصه دستور (SYNOPSIS)"
\f[B]rofi\f[R] [ \-show \f[I]mode\f[R] ]|[ \-dmenu ]|[ \-e \f[I]msg\f[R]
] CONFIGURATION
.SH "توضیحات (DESCRIPTION)"
\f[B]rofi\f[R] is an X11 pop\-up window switcher, run dialog, dmenu
replacement, and more.
It focuses on being fast to use and have minimal distraction.
It supports keyboard and mouse navigation, type to filter, tokenized
search and more.
.SH "نحوه استفاده (USAGE)"
\f[B]rofi\f[R]\[cq]s main functionality is to assist in your workflow,
allowing you to quickly switch between windows, start applications or
log into a remote machine via \f[B]ssh\f[R].
There are different \f[I]modes\f[R] for different types of actions.
\f[B]rofi\f[R] is a standalone application and should not be integrated
into scripts.
For integration into scripts it has a special mode that functions as a
(drop\-in) replacement for \f[B]dmenu(1)\f[R].
See emulating dmenu below.
.SS Running rofi
To launch \f[B]rofi\f[R] directly in a certain mode, specify a mode with
\f[B]rofi \-show <mode>\f[R].
To show the \f[B]drun\f[R] dialog:
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-show drun
.EE
.PP
A useful setup in minimalistic window managers is to combine
\f[B]drun\f[R], \f[B]run\f[R] with \f[B]window\f[R] mode:
.IP
.EX
  rofi \-show combi \-modes combi \-combi\-modes \[dq]window,drun,run\[dq]
.EE
.PP
In this setup it first list all open applications, then all installed
applications.
So if you type firefox and hit return, it will switch to the running
firefox, or launch it when it is not running.
.SS Emulating dmenu
\f[B]rofi\f[R] can emulate \f[B]dmenu(1)\f[R] (a dynamic menu for X11)
when launched with the \f[B]\-dmenu\f[R] flag.
.PP
For more information see \f[B]rofi\-dmenu(5)\f[R].
.SS Display Error message
\f[B]rofi\f[R] error dialog can also be called from the command line.
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-e \[dq]my message\[dq]
.EE
.PP
Markup support can be enabled, see CONFIGURATION options.
.SH "پیکربندی (CONFIGURATION)"
There are currently three methods of setting configuration options
(evaluated in order below):
.IP \[bu] 2
System configuration file (for example \f[B]/etc/rofi.rasi\f[R]).
It first checks \f[B]XDG_CONFIG_DIRS\f[R], and then \f[B]SYSCONFDIR\f[R]
(that is passed at compile time).
It loads the first config file it finds, it does not merge multiple
system configuration files.
.IP \[bu] 2
Rasi theme file: The new \f[I]theme\f[R] format can be used to set
configuration values.
.IP \[bu] 2
Command\-line options: Arguments passed to \f[B]rofi\f[R].
.PP
To get a template config file, run: \f[B]rofi \-dump\-config >
config.rasi\f[R]
.PP
This will contain (commented) all current configuration options,
modified options are uncommented.
.PP
To get a template config file that sets the icon\-theme run: \f[B]rofi
\-icon\-theme hicolor \-dump\-config\f[R].
.PP
It is \f[B]strongly\f[R] recommended to use this as a starting point for
your configuration.
.PP
An empty configuration section in the config file looks like:
.IP
.EX
configuration {
 \f[B]// set config options here\f[R]
}
.EE
.PP
Most of the configuration options mentioned below (beside options like
\f[B]\-show\f[R], \f[B]\-dump\-config\f[R] that apply to a single run)
can be set here.
.PP
For example to set the dpi value to 72:
.IP
.EX
configuration {
 dpi: 72;
}
.EE
.PP
The configuration system supports the following types:
.IP \[bu] 2
string
.IP \[bu] 2
integer (signed and unsigned)
.IP \[bu] 2
char
.IP \[bu] 2
boolean
.IP \[bu] 2
lists
.PP
For the syntax of these options, see the \f[B]rofi\-theme(5)\f[R]
manpage.
.PP
For use on the command line, Boolean options have a non\-default
command\-line syntax.
Example to enable option X:
.IP
.EX
    \-X
.EE
.PP
To disable option X:
.IP
.EX
    \-no\-X
.EE
.PP
Below is a list of the most important options:
.SS General
.TP
\f[B]\-help\f[R]
The help option shows the full list of command\-line options and the
current set values.
These include dynamic (run\-time generated) options.
.TP
\f[B]\-info\f[R]
The info option shows useful information to include when reporting an
issue.
This includes compile time options, detected monitors and more.
Please check the output for any personal information before posting
online.
.TP
\f[B]\-version\f[R]
Show the \f[B]rofi\f[R] version and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-dump\-config\f[R]
Dump the current active configuration, in rasi format, to stdout and
exit.
Information about the rasi format can be found in the
\f[B]rofi\-theme(5)\f[R] manpage.
.TP
\f[B]\-dump\-theme\f[R]
Dump the current active theme, in rasi format, to stdout and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-rasi\-validate\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Try to parse the file and return 0 when successful, non\-zero when
failed.
.TP
\f[B]\-list\-keybindings\f[R]
List all known keybindings without trying to parse them.
This can be used to look for duplicate bindings.
.TP
\f[B]\-threads\f[R] \f[I]num\f[R]
Specify the number of threads \f[B]rofi\f[R] should use:
.RS
.IP \[bu] 2
0: Autodetect the number of supported hardware threads.
.IP \[bu] 2
1: Disable threading
.IP \[bu] 2
2..n: Specify the maximum number of threads to use in the thread pool.
.PP
Default: Autodetect
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-display\f[R] \f[I]display\f[R]
The X server to contact.
Default is \f[B]$DISPLAY\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-dmenu\f[R]
Run \f[B]rofi\f[R] in dmenu mode.
This allows for interactive scripts.
In \f[B]dmenu\f[R] mode, \f[B]rofi\f[R] reads from STDIN, and output to
STDOUT.
A simple example, displaying three pre\-defined options:
.RS
.IP
.EX
    echo \-e \[dq]Option #1\[rs]nOption #2\[rs]nOption #3\[dq] \f[B]|\f[R] rofi \-dmenu
.EE
.PP
Or get the options from a script:
.IP
.EX
    \[ti]/my_script.sh \f[B]|\f[R] rofi \-dmenu
.EE
.PP
See the \f[B]rofi\-dmenu(5)\f[R] manpage for more information.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-show\f[R] \f[I]mode\f[R]
Open \f[B]rofi\f[R] in a certain mode.
Available modes are \f[B]window\f[R], \f[B]run\f[R], \f[B]drun\f[R],
\f[B]ssh\f[R], \f[B]combi\f[R].
The special argument \f[B]keys\f[R] can be used to open a searchable
list of supported key bindings (see the \f[B]rofi\-keys(5)\f[R] manpage)
.RS
.PP
To show the run\-dialog:
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-show run
.EE
.PP
If \f[B]\-show\f[R] is the last option passed to rofi, the first enabled
modes is shown.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-modes\f[R] \f[I]mode1,mode2\f[R]
Specify an ordered, comma\-separated list of modes to enable.
Enabled modes can be changed at runtime.
Default key is \f[B]Ctrl+Tab\f[R].
If no modes are specified, all configured modes will be enabled.
To only show the \f[B]run\f[R] and \f[B]ssh\f[R] launcher:
.RS
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-modes \[dq]run,ssh\[dq] \-show run
.EE
.PP
Custom modes can be added using the internal \f[B]script\f[R] mode.
Each such mode has two parameters:
.IP
.EX
<name>:<script>
.EE
.PP
Example: Have a mode called `Workspaces' using the
\f[B]i3_switch_workspaces.sh\f[R] script:
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-modes \[dq]window,run,ssh,Workspaces:i3_switch_workspaces.sh\[dq] \-show Workspaces
.EE
.PP
Notes: The i3 window manager dislikes commas in the command when
specifying an exec command.
For that case, \f[B]#\f[R] can be used as a separator.
.PP
\f[B]TIP\f[R]: The name is allowed to contain spaces:
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-modes \[dq]My File Browser:fb.sh\[dq] \-show \[dq]My File Browser\[dq]
.EE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-case\-sensitive\f[R]
Start in case\-sensitive mode.
This option can be changed at run\-time using the
\f[B]\-kb\-toggle\-case\-sensitivity\f[R] key binding.
.TP
\f[B]\-case\-smart\f[R]
Start in case\-smart mode behave like vim\[cq]s \f[B]smartcase\f[R],
which determines case\-sensitivity by input.
When enabled, this will suppress \f[B]\-case\-sensitive\f[R] config.
.TP
\f[B]\-cycle\f[R]
Cycle through the result list.
Default is `true'.
.TP
\f[B]\-filter\f[R] \f[I]filter\f[R]
Filter the list by setting text in input bar to \f[I]filter\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-config\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Load an alternative configuration file.
.TP
\f[B]\-cache\-dir\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Directory that is used to place temporary files, like history.
.TP
\f[B]\-scroll\-method\f[R] \f[I]method\f[R]
Select the scrolling method.
0: Per page, 1: continuous.
.TP
\f[B]\-normalize\-match\f[R]
Normalize the string before matching, so \f[B]o\f[R] will match
\f[B]ö\f[R], and \f[B]é\f[R] matches \f[B]e\f[R].
This is not a perfect implementation, but works.
For now, it disables highlighting of the matched part.
.TP
\f[B]\-no\-lazy\-grab\f[R]
Disables lazy grab, this forces the keyboard being grabbed before gui is
shown.
.TP
\f[B]\-no\-plugins\f[R]
Disable plugin loading.
.TP
\f[B]\-plugin\-path\f[R] \f[I]directory\f[R]
Specify the directory where \f[B]rofi\f[R] should look for plugins.
.TP
\f[B]\-show\-icons\f[R]
Show application icons in \f[B]drun\f[R] and \f[B]window\f[R] modes.
.TP
\f[B]\-icon\-theme\f[R]
Specify icon theme to be used.
If not specified default theme from DE is used, \f[I]Adwaita\f[R] and
\f[I]gnome\f[R] themes act as fallback themes.
.TP
\f[B]\-markup\f[R]
Use Pango markup to format output wherever possible.
.TP
\f[B]\-normal\-window\f[R]
Make \f[B]rofi\f[R] react like a normal application window.
Useful for scripts like Clerk that are basically an application.
.TP
\f[B]\-transient\-window\f[R]
Make \f[B]rofi\f[R] react like a modal dialog that is transient to the
currently focused window.
Useful when you use a keyboard shortcut to run and show on the window
you are working with.
.TP
\f[B]\-[no\-]steal\-focus\f[R]
Make rofi steal focus on launch and restore close to window that held it
when launched.
.TP
\f[B]\-refilter\-timeout\-limit\f[R]
The time (in ms) boundary filter may take before switch from instant to
delayed filter mode.
.RS
.PP
Default: 300
.PP
A fallback icon can be specified for each mode:
.IP
.EX
configuration {
    <mode>{
      fallback\-icon: \[dq]<icon name>\[dq];
    }
}
.EE
.PP
Example
.IP
.EX
configuration {
    run,drun {
      fallback\-icon: \[dq]application\-x\-addon\[dq];
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.SS Matching
.TP
\f[B]\-matching\f[R] \f[I]method\f[R]
Specify the matching algorithm used.
Currently, the following methods are supported:
.RS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]normal\f[R]: match the int string
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]regex\f[R]: match a regex input
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]glob\f[R]: match a glob pattern
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]fuzzy\f[R]: do a fuzzy match
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]prefix\f[R]: match prefix
.PP
Default: \f[I]normal\f[R]
.PP
Multiple matching methods can be specified in a comma separated list.
The matching up/down keybinding allows cycling through at runtime.
.PP
Note: glob matching might be slow for larger lists
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-tokenize\f[R]
Tokenize the input.
.TP
\f[B]\-drun\-categories\f[R] \f[I]category1\f[R],\f[I]category2\f[R]
Only show desktop files that are present in the listed categories.
.TP
\f[B]\-drun\-exclude\-categories\f[R] \f[I]category1\f[R],\f[I]category2\f[R]
Exclude desktop files that are present in the listed categories.
.TP
\f[B]\-drun\-match\-fields\f[R] \f[I]field1\f[R],\f[I]field2\f[R],\&...
When using \f[B]drun\f[R], match only with the specified Desktop entry
fields.
The different fields are:
.RS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]name\f[R]: the application\[cq]s name
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]generic\f[R]: the application\[cq]s generic name
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]exec\f[R]: the application\[cq]s executable
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]categories\f[R]: the application\[cq]s categories
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]comment\f[R]: the application comment
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]all\f[R]: all the above
.PP
Default: \f[I]name,generic,exec,categories,keywords\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-drun\-display\-format\f[R]
The format string for the \f[B]drun\f[R] dialog:
.RS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]name\f[R]: the application\[cq]s name
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]generic\f[R]: the application\[cq]s generic name
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]exec\f[R]: the application\[cq]s executable
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]categories\f[R]: the application\[cq]s categories
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]comment\f[R]: the application comment
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]url\f[R]: The url in case of a link type desktop file
.PP
Pango markup can be used to formatting the output.
.PP
Default: \f[B]{name} [<span weight=\[aq]light\[aq]
size=\[aq]small\[aq]><i>({generic})</i></span>]\f[R]
.PP
Note: Only fields enabled in \f[B]\-drun\-match\-fields\f[R] can be used
in the format string.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-[no\-]drun\-show\-actions\f[R]
Show actions present in the Desktop files.
.RS
.PP
Default: false
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-window\-match\-fields\f[R] \f[I]field1\f[R],\f[I]field2\f[R],\&...
When using window mode, match only with the specified fields.
The different fields are:
.RS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]title\f[R]: window\[cq]s title
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]class\f[R]: window\[cq]s class
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]role\f[R]: window\[cq]s role
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]name\f[R]: window\[cq]s name
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]desktop\f[R]: window\[cq]s current desktop
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]all\f[R]: all the above
.PP
For Wayland, the list of accepted fields is different:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]title\f[R]: window\[cq]s title
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]app\-id\f[R]: Wayland Application ID or XWayland window\[cq]s class
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]class\f[R]: same as app\-id
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]all\f[R]: all of the above
.PP
Default: \f[I]all\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-matching\-negate\-char\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Set the character used to negate the query (i.e.\ if it does
\f[B]not\f[R] match the next keyword).
Set to `' to disable.
It takes the first ASCII character from the string.
.RS
.PP
Default: `\-'
.RE
.SS Filtered menu sort
.TP
\f[B]\-[no]\-sort\f[R]
Enable, disable sort for filtered menu.
This setting can be changed at runtime (see
\f[B]\-kb\-toggle\-sort\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]\-sorting\-method\f[R] `method' to specify the sort method.
There are 2 methods:
.RS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]levenshtein\f[R] (Default)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]fzf\f[R]
.RE
.SS Layout and Theming
\f[B]IMPORTANT:\f[R] In newer \f[B]rofi\f[R] releases, all the theming
options have been moved into the new theme format.
They are no longer normal \f[B]rofi\f[R] options that can be passed
directly on the command line (there are too many).
Small snippets can be passed on the command line: \f[B]rofi \-theme\-str
\[aq]window {width: 50%;}\[aq]\f[R] to override a single setting.
They are merged into the current theme.
They can also be appended at the end of the \f[B]rofi\f[R] config file
to override parts of the theme.
.PP
Most of the following options are \f[B]deprecated\f[R] and should not be
used.
Please use the new theme format to customize \f[B]rofi\f[R].
More information about the new format can be found in the
\f[B]rofi\-theme(5)\f[R] manpage.
.TP
\f[B]\-location\f[R]
Specify where the window should be located.
The numbers map to the following locations on screen:
.RS
.IP
.EX
      1 2 3
      8 0 4
      7 6 5
.EE
.PP
Default: \f[I]0\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-fixed\-num\-lines\f[R]
Keep a fixed number of visible lines.
.TP
\f[B]\-sidebar\-mode\f[R]
Open in sidebar\-mode.
In this mode, a list of all enabled modes is shown at the bottom (See
\f[B]\-modes\f[R] option).
To show sidebar, use:
.RS
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-show run \-sidebar\-mode 
.EE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-hover\-select\f[R]
Automatically select the entry the mouse is hovering over.
This option is best combined with custom mouse bindings.
To utilize hover\-select and accept an entry in a single click, use:
.RS
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-show run \-hover\-select \-me\-select\-entry \[aq]\[aq] \-me\-accept\-entry MousePrimary
.EE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-eh\f[R] \f[I]number\f[R]
Set row height (in chars) Default: \f[I]1\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\-auto\-select\f[R]
When one entry is left, automatically select it.
.TP
\f[B]\-m\f[R] \f[I]num\f[R], \f[B]\-m\f[R] \f[I]name\f[R], \f[B]\-monitor\f[R] \f[I]num\f[R], \f[B]\-monitor\f[R] \f[I]name\f[R]
Select monitor to display \f[B]rofi\f[R] on.
It accepts as input: \f[I]primary\f[R] (if primary output is set), the
\f[I]xrandr\f[R] output name, or integer number (in order of detection).
Negative numbers are handled differently:
.RS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\-1\f[R]: the currently focused monitor.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\-2\f[R]: the currently focused window (that is, \f[B]rofi\f[R]
will be displayed on top of the focused window).
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\-3\f[R]: Position of mouse (overrides the location setting to get
normal context menu behavior.)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\-4\f[R]: the monitor with the focused window.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\-5\f[R]: the monitor that shows the mouse pointer.
.PP
Default: \f[I]\-5\f[R]
.PP
See \f[B]rofi \-h\f[R] output for the detected monitors, their position,
and size.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-theme\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Path to theme file, or name of an installed theme.
See \f[B]rofi\-theme(5)\f[R] manpage on how themes are resolved.
.TP
\f[B]\-theme\-str\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Allow theme parts to be specified on the command line as an override.
.RS
.PP
For example:
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-theme\-str \[aq]#window { fullscreen: true; }\[aq]
.EE
.PP
This option can be specified multiple times.
This is now the method to tweak the theme via the command line.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-dpi\f[R] \f[I]number\f[R]
Override the default DPI setting.
.RS
.PP
On X11:
.IP \[bu] 2
If set to \f[B]0\f[R], it tries to auto\-detect based on X11 screen size
(similar to i3 and GTK).
.IP \[bu] 2
If set to \f[B]1\f[R], it tries to auto\-detect based on the size of the
monitor that \f[B]rofi\f[R] is displayed on (similar to latest Qt 5).
.PP
On Wayland:
.IP \[bu] 2
If set to \f[B]0\f[R] or \f[B]1\f[R], it tries to auto\-detect if there
is one monitor, or if there is a \f[B]\-monitor\f[R] name specified.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-selected\-row\f[R] \f[I]selected row\f[R]
Select a certain row.
.RS
.PP
Default: \f[I]0\f[R]
.RE
.SS PATTERN setting
.TP
\f[B]\-terminal\f[R]
Specify which terminal to start.
.RS
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-terminal xterm
.EE
.PP
Pattern: \f[I]{terminal}\f[R]
.PP
Default: \f[I]x\-terminal\-emulator\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-ssh\-client\f[R] \f[I]client\f[R]
Override the used \f[B]ssh\f[R] client.
.RS
.PP
Pattern: \f[I]{ssh\-client}\f[R]
.PP
Default: \f[I]ssh\f[R]
.RE
.SS SSH settings
.TP
\f[B]\-ssh\-command\f[R] \f[I]cmd\f[R]
Set the command to execute when starting an ssh session.
The pattern \f[I]{host}\f[R] is replaced by the selected ssh entry.
.RS
.PP
Pattern: \f[I]{ssh\-client}\f[R]
.PP
Default: \f[I]{terminal} \-e {ssh\-client} {host}\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-parse\-hosts\f[R]
Parse the \f[B]/etc/hosts\f[R] file for entries.
.RS
.PP
Default: \f[I]disabled\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-[no\-]parse\-known\-hosts\f[R]
Parse the \f[B]\[ti]/.ssh/known_hosts\f[R] file for entries.
.RS
.PP
Default: \f[I]enabled\f[R]
.RE
.SS Run settings
.TP
\f[B]\-run\-command\f[R] \f[I]cmd\f[R]
Set command (\f[I]{cmd}\f[R]) to execute when running an application.
See \f[I]PATTERN\f[R].
.RS
.PP
Default: \f[I]{cmd}\f[R]
.PP
Example to run applications in a dedicated cgroup with systemd.
Requires a shell to escape and interpolate the unit name correctly.
.IP
.EX
\[dq]bash \-c \[aq]systemd\-run \-\-user \-\-unit=app\-rofi\-\[rs]$(systemd\-escape {cmd})\-\[rs]$RANDOM {cmd}\[aq]\[dq]
.EE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-run\-shell\-command\f[R] \f[I]cmd\f[R]
Set command to execute when running an application in a shell.
See \f[I]PATTERN\f[R].
.RS
.PP
Default: \f[I]{terminal} \-e {cmd}\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-run\-list\-command\f[R] \f[I]cmd\f[R]
If set, use an external tool to generate a list of executable commands.
Uses \f[B]run\-command\f[R].
.RS
.PP
Default: \f[I]{cmd}\f[R]
.RE
.SS Window switcher settings
.TP
\f[B]\-window\-format\f[R] \f[I]format\f[R]
Format what is being displayed for windows.
.RS
.PP
\f[I]format\f[R]: {field[:len]}
.PP
\f[I]field\f[R]:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]w\f[R]: desktop name
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]t\f[R]: title of window
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]n\f[R]: name
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]r\f[R]: role
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]c\f[R]: class
.PP
\f[I]len\f[R]: maximum field length (0 for auto\-size).
If length is negative, the entry will be unchanged.
If length is positive, the entry will be truncated or padded to fill
that length.
.PP
default: {w} {c} {t}
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-window\-command\f[R] \f[I]cmd\f[R]
Set command to execute on selected window for an alt action
(\f[B]\-kb\-accept\-alt\f[R]).
See \f[I]PATTERN\f[R].
.RS
.PP
Default: \f[I]\[lq]wmctrl \-i \-R {window}\[rq]\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-window\-thumbnail\f[R]
Show window thumbnail (if available) as icon in the window switcher.
.RS
.PP
You can stop rofi from exiting when closing a window (allowing multiple
to be closed in a row).
.IP
.EX
configuration {
  window {
      close\-on\-delete: false;
  }
}
.EE
.PP
You can hide the currently active window with the `hide\-active\-window'
setting:
.IP
.EX
configuration {
  window {
      hide\-active\-window: true;
  }
}
.EE
.PP
or pass \f[B]\-window\-hide\-active\-window true\f[R] on command line.
.PP
You can prefer the icon theme above the window set icon with the
`prefer\-icon\-theme' setting:
.IP
.EX
configuration {
  window {
      prefer\-icon\-theme: true;
  }
}
.EE
.PP
or pass \f[B]\-window\-prefer\-icon\-theme true\f[R] on command line.
.RE
.SS Combi settings
.TP
\f[B]\-combi\-modes\f[R] \f[I]mode1\f[R],\f[I]mode2\f[R]
The modes to combine in combi mode.
For syntax to \f[B]\-combi\-modes\f[R], see \f[B]\-modes\f[R].
To get one merge view, of \f[B]window\f[R],\f[B]run\f[R], and
\f[B]ssh\f[R]:
.RS
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-show combi \-combi\-modes \[dq]window,run,ssh\[dq] \-modes combi
.EE
.PP
\f[B]NOTE\f[R]: The i3 window manager dislikes commas in the command
when specifying an exec command.
For that case, \f[B]#\f[R] can be used as a separator.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-combi\-display\-format\f[R]
The format string for entries in the \f[B]combi\f[R] dialog:
.RS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]mode\f[R]: the mode display name
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]text\f[R]: the entry text
.PP
Pango markup can be used to formatting the output.
.PP
Default: {mode} {text}
.PP
Note: This setting is ignored if \f[B]combi\-hide\-mode\-prefix\f[R] is
enabled.
.RE
.SS History
.TP
\f[B]\-[no\-]disable\-history\f[R]
Disable or re\-enable history
.TP
\f[B]\-max\-history\-size\f[R] \f[I]number\f[R]
Maximum number of entries to store in history.
Defaults to 25.
(WARNING: can cause slowdowns when set too high)
.SS Message dialog
.TP
\f[B]\-e\f[R] \f[I]message\f[R]
Pops up a message dialog (used internally for showing errors) with
\f[I]message\f[R].
Message can be multi\-line.
.RS
.PP
Passing \f[B]\-e \-\f[R] reads (blocking) from standard in and displays
this.
.RE
.SS File browser settings
File browser behavior can be controlled via the following options:
.IP
.EX
configuration {
   filebrowser {
      \f[I]/** Directory the file browser starts in. */\f[R]
      directory: \[dq]/some/directory\[dq];
      \f[I]/**\f[R]
\f[I]        * Sorting method. Can be set to:\f[R]
\f[I]        *   \- \[dq]name\[dq]\f[R]
\f[I]        *   \- \[dq]mtime\[dq] (modification time)\f[R]
\f[I]        *   \- \[dq]atime\[dq] (access time)\f[R]
\f[I]        *   \- \[dq]ctime\[dq] (change time)\f[R]
\f[I]        */\f[R]
      sorting\-method: \[dq]name\[dq];
      \f[I]/** Group directories before files. */\f[R]
      directories\-first: true;
      \f[I]/** Show hidden files. */\f[R]
      show\-hidden: false;
      \f[I]/** return 1 on cancel. */\f[R]
      cancel\-returns\-1: true;
      \f[I]/** command */\f[R]
      command: \[dq]xdg\-open\[dq];
   }
}
.EE
.PP
These options can also be passed on the commandline, for example:
.IP
.EX
rofi \-filebrowser\-cancel\-returns\-1 true \-show filebrowser
.EE
.PP
The \f[B]show\-hidden\f[R] can also be triggered with the
\f[B]kb\-delete\-entry\f[R] keybinding.
.SS Recursive Browser settings
Recursive file browser behavior can be controlled via the following
options:
.IP
.EX
configuration {
   recursivebrowser {
      \f[I]/** Directory the file browser starts in. */\f[R]
      directory: \[dq]/some/directory\[dq];
      \f[I]/** return 1 on cancel. */\f[R]
      cancel\-returns\-1: true;
      \f[I]/** filter entries using regex */\f[R]
      filter\-regex: \[dq](.*cache.*|.*\[rs].o)\[dq];
      \f[I]/** command */\f[R]
      command: \[dq]xdg\-open\[dq];
   }
}
.EE
.SS Entry history
The number of previous inputs for the entry box can be modified by
setting max\-history on the entry box.
.IP
.EX
configuration {
    entry  {
        max\-history: 30;
    }
}
.EE
.PP
By default the file is stored in the systems cache directory, in a file
called \f[B]rofi\-entry\-history.txt\f[R].
.SS Other
.TP
\f[B]\-drun\-use\-desktop\-cache\f[R]
Build and use a cache with the content of desktop files.
Usable for systems with slow hard drives.
.TP
\f[B]\-drun\-reload\-desktop\-cache\f[R]
If \f[B]drun\-use\-desktop\-cache\f[R] is enabled, rebuild a cache with
the content of desktop files.
.TP
\f[B]\-drun\-url\-launcher\f[R] \f[I]command\f[R]
Command to open a Desktop Entry that is a Link.
.TP
\f[B]\-pid\f[R] \f[I]path\f[R]
Make \f[B]rofi\f[R] create a pid file and check this on startup.
The pid file prevents multiple \f[B]rofi\f[R] instances from running
simultaneously.
This is useful when running \f[B]rofi\f[R] from a key\-binding daemon.
.TP
\f[B]\-replace\f[R]
If rofi is already running, based on pid file, try to kill that
instance.
.TP
\f[B]\-display\-{mode}\f[R] \f[I]string\f[R]
Set the name to use for mode.
This is used as prompt and in combi\-browser.
.RS
.PP
It is now preferred to use the configuration file:
.IP
.EX
configuration {
  {mode} {
    display\-name: *string*;
  }
}
.EE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-[no\-]click\-to\-exit\f[R]
Click the mouse outside the \f[B]rofi\f[R] window to exit.
.RS
.PP
Default: \f[I]enabled\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-global\-kb\f[R] \f[B]\-no\-global\-kb\f[R]
(wayland) Override the compositor\[cq]s keybindings, so that
\f[B]rofi\f[R] can re\-use them.
.RS
.PP
Default: \f[I]disabled\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\-xserver\-i300\-workaround\f[R]
Workaround for bug in Xserver.
See issue #611 and #1642 on the rofi issue tracker.
.RS
.PP
Default: \f[I]disabled\f[R]
.RE
.SH PATTERN
To launch commands (for example, when using the ssh launcher), the user
can enter the used command\-line.
The following keys can be used that will be replaced at runtime:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]{host}\f[R]: the host to connect to
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]{terminal}\f[R]: the configured terminal (see \-terminal)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]{ssh\-client}\f[R]: the configured ssh client (see \-ssh\-client)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]{cmd}\f[R]: the command to execute
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]{window}\f[R]: the window ID of the selected window (in
\f[B]window\-command\f[R])
.PP
It processes the string as follows: \f[B]{key}\f[R] is replaced by its
value, if \f[B]{key}\f[R] is not set it is removed.
If the \f[B]{key}\f[R] is in between \f[B][]\f[R] all the text between
\f[B][]\f[R] is removed if \f[B]{key}\f[R] is not set.
Otherwise key is replaced and the \f[B][]\f[R] are removed.
.PP
For example: \f[B]{ssh\-client} [\-p {port}] {host}\f[R]
.SH THEMING
Please see \f[B]rofi\-theme(5)\f[R] manpage for more information on
theming.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
Please see the \f[B]rofi\-keys(5)\f[R] manpage for the keybindings and
how to set them up.
.PP
The keybinding can also be used for actions, when the action is executed
the mentioned keystroke is inserted:
.SS Timeout
You can configure an action to be taken when rofi has not been
interacted with for a certain amount of seconds.
You can specify a keybinding to trigger after X seconds.
.IP
.EX
configuration {
  timeout {
      delay:  15;
      action: \[dq]kb\-cancel\[dq];
  }
}
.EE
.SS Input change
When the input of the textbox changes:
.IP
.EX
configuration {
  inputchange {
      action: \[dq]kb\-row\-first\[dq];
  }
}
.EE
.SH Available Modes
.SS window
Show a list of all the windows and allow switching between them.
Pressing the \f[B]delete\-entry\f[R] binding (\f[B]shift\-delete\f[R])
will close the window.
Pressing the \f[B]accept\-custom\f[R] binding (\f[B]control\-enter\f[R]
or \f[B]shift\-enter\f[R]) will run a command on the window.
(See option \f[B]window\-command\f[R] );
.PP
If there is no match, it will try to launch the input.
.SS windowcd
Shows a list of the windows on the current desktop and allows switching
between them.
Pressing the \f[B]delete\-entry\f[R] binding (\f[B]shift\-delete\f[R])
will kill the window.
Pressing the \f[B]accept\-custom\f[R] binding (\f[B]control\-enter\f[R]
or \f[B]shift\-enter\f[R]) will run a command on the window.
(See option \f[B]window\-command\f[R] );
.PP
If there is no match, it will try to launch the input.
.SS run
Shows a list of executables in \f[B]$PATH\f[R] and can launch them
(optional in a terminal).
.IP \[bu] 2
Pressing the \f[B]delete\-entry\f[R] binding (\f[B]shift\-delete\f[R])
will remove this entry from the run history.
.IP \[bu] 2
Pressing the \f[B]accept\-custom\f[R] binding (\f[B]control\-enter\f[R])
will run the command as entered in the entry box.
.IP \[bu] 2
Pressing the \f[B]accept\-alt\f[R] binding (\f[B]shift\-enter\f[R]) will
run the command in a terminal.
.PP
When pressing the \f[B]mode\-complete\f[R] binding
(\f[B]Control\-l\f[R]), you can use the File Browser mode to launch the
application with a file as the first argument.
.SS drun
Same as the \f[B]run\f[R] launches, but the list is created from the
installed desktop files.
It automatically launches them in a terminal if specified in the Desktop
File.
.IP \[bu] 2
Pressing the \f[B]delete\-entry\f[R] binding (\f[B]shift\-delete\f[R])
will remove this entry from the run history.
.IP \[bu] 2
Pressing the \f[B]accept\-custom\f[R] binding (\f[B]control\-enter\f[R])
will run the command as entered in the entry box.
.IP \[bu] 2
Pressing the \f[B]accept\-alt\f[R] binding (\f[B]shift\-enter\f[R]) will
run the command in a terminal.
.PP
When pressing the \f[B]mode\-complete\f[R] binding
(\f[B]Control\-l\f[R]), you can use the File Browser mode to launch the
application passing a file as argument if specified in the desktop file.
.PP
The DRUN mode tries to follow the \c
.UR https://freedesktop.org/wiki/Specifications/desktop-entry-spec/
XDG Desktop Entry Specification
.UE \c
\ and should be compatible with applications using this standard.
Some applications create invalid desktop files, \f[B]rofi\f[R] will
discard these entries.
See the debugging section for more info on DRUN mode, this will print
why desktop files are discarded.
.PP
There are a few advanced options to tweak the behaviour:
.IP
.EX
configuration {
   drun {
      \f[I]/** Scan the current users desktop for desktop files. */\f[R]
      scan\-desktop: true;
      \f[I]/** Parse user desktop files. */\f[R]
      parse\-user:   true;
      \f[I]/** Parse system desktop files. */\f[R]
      parse\-system: false;
      \f[I]/** Disable DBusActivatable */\f[R]
      DBusActivatable: false;
   }
}
.EE
.SS ssh
Shows a list of SSH targets based on your \f[B]ssh\f[R] config file, and
allows to quickly \f[B]ssh\f[R] into them.
.SS keys
Shows a searchable list of key bindings.
.SS script
Allows custom scripted Modes to be added, see the
\f[B]rofi\-script(5)\f[R] manpage for more information.
.SS combi
Combines multiple modes in one list.
Specify which modes are included with the \f[B]\-combi\-modes\f[R]
option.
.PP
When using the combi mode, a \f[I]!bang\f[R] can be used to filter the
results by modes.
All modes that match the bang as a prefix are included.
For example, say you have specified \f[B]\-combi\-modes
run,window,windowcd\f[R].
If your query begins with the bang \f[B]!w\f[R], only results from the
\f[B]window\f[R] and \f[B]windowcd\f[R] modes are shown, even if the
rest of the input text would match results from \f[B]run\f[R].
.PP
If no match, the input is handled by the first combined modes.
.SH FAQ
.SS The text in the window switcher is not nicely aligned
Try using a mono\-space font or tabs + the tab\-stops setting..
.SS The window is completely black
Check quotes used on the command\-line: you might have used
\f[B]\[lq]\f[R] (\[lq]smart quotes\[rq]) instead of \f[B]\[dq]\f[R]
(\[lq]machine quotes\[rq]).
.SS What does the icon in the top right show?
The indicator shows:
.IP \[bu] 2
\[ga]\[ga] Case insensitive and no sorting.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\-\f[R] Case sensitivity enabled, no sorting.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]+\f[R] Case insensitive and Sorting enabled
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]±\f[R] Sorting and Case sensitivity enabled\[rq]
.SS Why do I see different icons for run,drun and window mode
Each of these modes uses different methods of resolving the icon:
.IP \[bu] 2
Window: It first uses the icon that the application exposes via the X11
Server, if none is set it does a lookup of the window Class name in the
icon theme.
.IP \[bu] 2
drun: It uses the icon set in the desktop file.
.IP \[bu] 2
run: It does a lookup using the executable name.
.SH "مثال‌ها (EXAMPLES)"
Some basic usage examples of \f[B]rofi\f[R]:
.PP
Show the run dialog:
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-modes run \-show run
.EE
.PP
Show the run dialog, and allow switching to Desktop File run dialog
(\f[B]drun\f[R]):
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-modes run,drun \-show run
.EE
.PP
Combine the run and Desktop File run dialog (\f[B]drun\f[R]):
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-modes combi \-show combi \-combi\-modes run,drun
.EE
.PP
Combine the run and Desktop File run dialog (\f[B]drun\f[R]), and allow
switching to window switcher:
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-modes combi,window \-show combi \-combi\-modes run,drun
.EE
.PP
Pop up a text message claiming that this is the end:
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-e \[dq]This is the end\[dq]
.EE
.PP
Pop up a text message in red, bold font claiming that this is still the
end:
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-e \[dq]<span color=\[aq]red\[aq]><b>This is still the end</b></span>\[dq] \-markup
.EE
.PP
Show all key bindings:
.IP
.EX
    rofi \-show keys
.EE
.SH i3
In \c
.UR http://i3wm.org/
i3
.UE \c
\ you want to bind \f[B]rofi\f[R] to be launched on key release.
Otherwise, it cannot grab the keyboard.
See also the i3 \c
.UR http://i3wm.org/docs/userguide.html
manual
.UE \c
:
.PP
Some tools (such as \f[B]import\f[R] or \f[B]xdotool\f[R]) might be
unable to run upon a KeyPress event, because the keyboard/pointer is
still grabbed.
For these situations, the \f[B]\-\-release\f[R] flag can be used, as it
will execute the command after the keys have been released.
.SH Hyprland
Hyprland\[cq]s animations make the launching of \f[B]Rofi\f[R] feel
slower then needed.
To avoid this, add the following rule to your hyprland.conf file:
.IP
.EX
layerrule = noanim,\[ha](rofi)$
.EE
.PP
This disables the animations on the \f[B]Rofi\f[R] window.
